[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-05", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 05 — a malformed question, a refuted justification, and the write side", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-05.edn", :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 05 - a malformed question, a refuted justification, and the write side\n\n> ⚠ **CORRECTED by iteration 07 (2026-09-09).** Section E's transfer argument\n> — \"One link per tx, and a tx is a commit ... 0.50x is the figure for it\" —\n> quoted root ADR-2608021000's depth, which describes the shape this codebase\n> LEFT: `novelty-segment-size` is 16, so the novelty chain is **ceil(tx/16)**\n> deep, i.e. FOUR links at the fold threshold, not 64. The crossing is real (the\n> four surviving segments are written by four different commits, measured in\n> kotobase-peer `novelty_chain_depth_test`), so the 0.50x RATIO transfers. The\n> depth does not, and the window prediction below is 16x too generous as a\n> statement about production.\n\n\n> Seeds from iteration 04: hold the pack opens too; walk a real novelty chain;\n> measure the write side, which nothing had measured at all.\n\n## D. The question was malformed, and the harness said 6.00x\n\nIteration 04 predicted that holding the pack opens as well as the catalog makes\npacked cost N and beat per-object's 2N on every workload, and said it needed the\nmeasurement and not the arithmetic. It needed neither: it needed the comparison\nto be fair.\n\n    query | per-object  disc  fetch | packed  catalog  reads     vs\n        1          12      6      6      12        2     10   1.00x\n        2           6      0      6       0        0      0   6.00x\n        3           6      0      6       0        0      0   6.00x\n\nThe packed arm costs ZERO on the second query. That is not cheap, it is nothing\ncrossing the network -- ayatori.pack's own README records: re-read through the\ngetter, 0, the getter's memo. A held open pack memoises decoded blocks.\n\nThe per-object arm was given a provider cache and NOT a block memo. So 6.00x is\nthe ratio between an arm that remembers blocks and an arm that does not, which\nis a statement about this file and not about packing. Memoise both and both are\n0: a tie, about nothing.\n\n**What survives: for REPEAT queries there is no comparison to make, and the cold\nrule from iteration 04 stands unchanged.** Iteration 04's beats-everywhere was an\nartifact of warming one arm, and it took building it to see that.\n\n## E. The chain walk refutes the ADR's own justification\n\nIteration 04 section C read every block of ONE snapshot and packing won\n1.43x-1.54x. That is not a chain: the CIDs were known upfront and lived in few\npacks. This walks the real thing -- the commit chain, where commit N's block\nnames commit N-1 and you cannot ask for N-1 until you have decoded N.\n\n    commits | per-object | packed  catalog  reads  opened      vs\n          2           4        8        2      6       2   0.50x\n          4           8       16        4     12       4   0.50x\n          8          16       32        8     24       8   0.50x\n         16          32       64       16     48      16   0.50x\n\n**A 2x LOSS at every length, exactly.** Under one commit one pack, a chain that\nruns backwards through commits touches exactly one block per pack: N/P = 1.00,\nthe worst ratio there is. Each link costs 2 (open) + 1 (block) + 1 (catalog) = 4\nwhere per-object costs 2.\n\nADR-2608160100 makes write locality the packing policy and cites this shape as\nthe reason: hydration's sequential term is 97% the novelty cons chain, width 1,\nprefetch impossible, and if they are in the same pack one Range GET gets them\nall. The mechanism is right. The grouping does not achieve it. A commit-scoped\npack cannot co-locate a chain whose links are, by construction, in different\ncommits.\n\n### It applies to the chain the ADR names — settled, not assumed\n\nThe chain measured above is the COMMIT chain. kotobase's is the novelty cons\nchain, so whether the finding transfers depended on one fact: do ITS links cross\ncommits? Root ADR-2608021000 answers it in its own words, describing unfolded\nnovelty as a cons chain of tx-link and rest-link where the next CID does not\nexist until the previous one is decoded -- width 1, and DEPTH EQUAL TO THE\nNUMBER OF UNFOLDED TRANSACTIONS.\n\nOne link per tx, and a tx is a commit. So the novelty chain crosses commits by\nconstruction, exactly like the commit chain measured here, and 0.50x is the\nfigure for it.\n\nThat ADR also supplies the window: default-fold-threshold is 64, described as\nthe most novelty a healthy graph carries between folds, and it measures novelty\nat 97% of hydrate's sequential round trips at that depth.\n\n**So the actionable form of E is a number: pack the NOVELTY WINDOW, not the\ncommit.** One pack spanning up to 64 unfolded txs turns 64 chain hops from\n2 x 64 = 128 per-object round trips into 2 (open) + 64 (blocks) + 1 (catalog)\n= 67, and N/P becomes 64 -- an order of magnitude above either crossover,\ninstead of the 1.00 a commit-scoped pack forces.\n\n## F. The write side, measured for the first time\n\n    commits  blocks | per-object | packed  objects      bytes  overhead\n          1       4     4 puts      1 puts       1      21166     +2.2%\n          2      14    14 puts      2 puts       2      64161     +2.2%\n          4      30    30 puts      4 puts       4     203676     +1.4%\n          8      67    67 puts      8 puts       8     673994     +1.0%\n         16     144   144 puts     16 puts      16    1831714     +0.7%\n\nOne PUT per commit instead of one per block: **144 puts become 16, a 9x\nreduction**, and the byte overhead SHRINKS as packs grow (+2.2% to +0.7%)\nbecause a pack's fixed header and index amortise over more blocks.\n\nThis is unambiguous and it is the one direction nothing had looked at. It is\nalso the direction where write locality is trivially the right grouping, because\nit is the only grouping a writer has.\n\n## Together, across five iterations\n\n    write             144 puts -> 16 puts        9.00x         packing wins\n    read one pack     every block of a snapshot  1.43x-1.54x   wins\n    read a chain      across commits, width 1    0.50x         loses\n    read a query      narrow, index-shaped       0.71x-1.00x   loses\n    read, repeat      both arms memoised         no comparison\n\nPacking is a write-side win by construction, and a read-side win only for reads\nthat follow the same locality the writer used. ADR-2608160100 is right that the\nlayout must be declared per backend; the measurement adds that it should be\ndeclared per ACCESS PATTERN, and that the pattern its own text cites is the one\nwhere commit-scoped packing loses 2x.\n\n## Seeds for iteration 06\n\n  - Build the read-locality pack: seal one along the novelty window rather than\n    along a commit, and re-run E. The prediction is N/P = 64 and it is now a\n    prediction with a number in it.\n  - Section D's real question, asked properly: give BOTH arms a block memo and\n    measure the cold path only, which is the only path where they differ.\n  - Everything measured so far is one host and an in-memory range server. The\n    figures are round trips, which transfer; the bytes are not a network\n    measurement, and have never been one in any iteration."}]
